<BaseLayout title="SEO query fanout workflow" description="Turn query-fanout branches into one bounded SEO page decision without publishing a URL for every variation." canonicalPath="/seo-query-fanout-workflow/" pageClass="reference-page" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <header class="reference-hero"><p class="mono-label">Workflow · SEO decision layer</p><h1>Turn query fanout into site work, not a URL factory.</h1><p>A query fanout is a map of plausible questions, intents, entities, and evidence needs. The SEO job begins after that map exists: identify the current page owner and choose the smallest defensible change.</p></header>
  <section class="reference-section split-section"><div><p class="mono-label">Upstream</p><h2>Analysis proposes branches.</h2><p><a href="https://ai-fanout.com/" rel="external">AI Fanout</a> owns the general planning protocol. Its branches are hypotheses—not hidden searches, verified demand, or instructions to publish.</p></div><div><p class="mono-label">Here</p><h2>Inventory determines the action.</h2><p>SEO Fanout compares those branches with real canonical pages, visible promises, evidence, journeys, and maintenance ownership. Common ownership is disclosed; neither site independently validates the other.</p></div></section>
  <section class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">Four-stage procedure</p><h2>A branch earns work only after an inventory check.</h2></div><ol class="method-list"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Declare one primary job</h3><p>Write the outcome a person must reach. Similar phrases serving the same outcome remain one job until evidence shows otherwise.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Preserve provenance</h3><p>Keep the original question, branch, intent, rationale, source suggestion, assumption, model label, and method version. A suggestion is not a verified source.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Inspect plausible page owners</h3><p>Record status, canonical, H1, promise, evidence state, coverage, next step, and maintenance owner. Use <a href="/page-inventory/">the inventory worksheet</a>; do not decide from slugs alone.</p></div></li><li><span>04</span><div><h3>Choose one bounded action</h3><p>Extend a page, add a section, merge intent-equivalent content, build an evidence asset, create one durable URL, or record no action.</p></div></li></ol></section>
  <section class="reference-section split-section"><div><h2>Evidence that can change the decision</h2><ul><li>Search Console query-to-page data from an authenticated property.</li><li>Analytics or conversion evidence tied to the page job.</li><li>Current primary documentation and firsthand observations.</li><li>A crawl showing canonicals, links, status, and duplicate ownership.</li></ul></div><div><h2>Signals that cannot decide alone</h2><ul><li>A keyword or branch appearing in a generated list.</li><li>A competitor having a dedicated URL.</li><li>A model suggesting a source type or entity.</li><li>A page title or URL containing similar wording.</li></ul></div></section>
  <section class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">Stop rule</p><h2>No action is a valid result.</h2></div><p>If one canonical page already owns the job, covers the important branches with credible current evidence, and provides the right next step, record the review and stop. Google’s people-first guidance favors useful, original content; it does not create a word-count or page-count target.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" rel="external">Google: helpful, reliable, people-first content <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p><a class="button-link" href="/tool/#explorer">Run the page decision <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section>
</BaseLayout>
